topics.description:
Whatever you put between `<Card>` and `</Card>` is passed to the component as the special prop **`children`**. This lets you build generic wrapper components (layouts, panels, modals, buttons) that do not know their content in advance.

```jsx
function Panel({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      {children}
    </section>
  );
}
```

### What can children be?

Text, a single element, an array of elements, a function (render prop), or nothing (`undefined`). Because the type varies, avoid assuming it is an array.

### Children utilities

`React.Children` has `map`, `count`, `toArray` and `only` helpers. They are useful but fragile, because they only see direct children and break when someone wraps them in a Fragment or another component. Prefer **composition** and explicit props (slots) for complex cases.

### Multiple slots

If you need several areas, pass JSX as normal props: `<Layout header={<Nav />} sidebar={<Menu />}>...</Layout>`.

### Why it matters

`children` is the base of composition in React: instead of configuring a component with many flags, you hand it the content you want inside.

